INT. BOWLING ALLEY - LANE 9 - NIGHT

Neon pins on the wall. Carpet printed with comets. The scoring monitor above lane 9 reads HAPPY BIRTHDAY NOOR in rainbow letters. Balls clatter down the lanes in the dim.

NOOR, 15, sits in a plastic chair with a paper crown tilted on her head. She holds a slice of cake like a plate she's not sure she's allowed to eat from.

WESLEY, 15, flips through the score sheet with studied boredom. BEANS, 14, bites a straw flat. PRISCILLA, 16, texts under the table, glancing up at the door every few seconds.

Nobody speaks. A ball thunders down the next lane, and all four of them watch it.

NOOR'S MOTHER, MRS. HAKIMI, 40s, in a work blazer, stands at the shoe counter signing a receipt. She doesn't look at the kids.

Behind the counter, RUDY, 60s, spritzes a pair of size sevens with disinfectant. He watches Noor the way a man watches weather.

WESLEY
You're up, Noor.

NOOR
I know.

WESLEY
It's your birthday. You go first.

NOOR
I said I know.

She doesn't move. She sets the cake on her knee. Priscilla's phone buzzes, and she slides it into her sleeve.

BEANS
Is she going to throw it or what?

NOOR
Who?

BEANS
Anyone.

PRISCILLA ^
(under her breath)
She's not coming.

NOOR
(not looking up)
Who's not coming?

WESLEY
Nobody. Throw the ball.

Noor rises. Takes a ten-pound ball from the rack. She holds it with both hands, studies the pins. Down the lane, the machine resets.

At the counter, Mrs. Hakimi turns. For a moment her eyes meet her daughter's. She lifts one hand in a half wave, then returns to the receipt.

Noor raises the ball. Draws back. Lets it go. It rolls hard and straight into the gutter. The monitor chirps: GUTTER.

No one says a word. Rudy sets down the disinfectant and presses the button that raises the bumpers.

Noor stays at the line. Her paper crown has slipped over one eye. She does not fix it.
